/// Chapter 21 of the guide, written from md-docs/21-i18n.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page I18nGuide(path: "/docs/guide/i18n", title: "Internationalisation", description: "Translation files, t() with placeholders and plurals, choosing and switching the locale, right-to-left, and locale-aware formatting.", layout: DocsShell("Internationalisation", chapter: t("guide.chapter", { n: "21" }), blurb: "One JSON file per locale, t() to read it, and a switch that flips the whole site — direction included.", source: "md-docs/21-i18n.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "setting-up", label: "Setting up"), Anchor(id: "starting-translated", label: "Starting translated"), Anchor(id: "t-key", label: "t(\"key\")"), Anchor(id: "switching-the-locale", label: "Switching the locale"), Anchor(id: "which-locale-a-page-opens-in", label: "Which locale a page opens in"), Anchor(id: "numbers-dates-and-the-locale", label: "Numbers, dates and the locale"), Anchor(id: "translating-a-markdown-page-or-a-md-page", label: "Translating a Markdown page or a .md page"), Anchor(id: "checklist", label: "Checklist"), Anchor(id: "the-translations-as-a-table", label: "The translations as a table")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/motion", prevLabel: "20. {t("ch.20")}", nextTo: "/docs/guide/accessibility", nextLabel: "22. {t("ch.22")}")) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("A site speaks several languages by keeping its text in one JSON file per locale and reading it with `t(\"key\")`. Switching the locale re-renders every translated string, flips the document to right-to-left where the language reads that way, and changes how `format` and `ago` spell numbers and dates.")

        Heading("Setting up", id: "setting-up").h2

        Markdown("In `webfluent.app.json`:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "i18n": {
    "default_locale": "en",
    "locales": ["en", "ar", "es"],
    "dir": "src/translations"
  }
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("One file per locale in that directory, flat keys with dots by convention:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "nav.home": "Home",
  "nav.about": "About",
  "welcome": "Welcome, {name}!",
  "items.one": "{count} item",
  "items.other": "{count} items",
  "actions.save": "Save"
}"#, title: "json")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "nav.home": "الرئيسية",
  "nav.about": "حول",
  "welcome": "أهلاً، {name}!",
  "items.zero": "لا عناصر",
  "items.one": "عنصر واحد",
  "items.two": "عنصران",
  "items.few": "{count} عناصر",
  "items.many": "{count} عنصراً",
  "items.other": "{count} عنصر",
  "actions.save": "حفظ"
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("A missing file is a build warning; a key missing from a locale falls back to the default locale, then to the key itself.")

        Heading("Starting translated", id: "starting-translated").h2

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf init my-app --locales en,ar")
            ])

        Markdown("starts a project — `spa` or `static` — in those languages, the first the default: the `i18n` config above, `meta.lang` the default, every visible string through `t(\"…\")`, a language switch with one entry per locale, and a file for each in `src/translations/`. The default's holds the starter's English, which its pages and tests are written in; Arabic holds the starter's Arabic; any other locale starts as the same English, for you to translate. Each locale is a language tag (`en`, `ar`, `pt-BR`); `--locales ar,en` makes Arabic the default, so the page is right to left from the first paint. From a program it is `webfluent::init::create_with(dir, name, &Options \{ template, locales \})`.")

        Heading("t(\"key\")", id: "t-key").h2

        CodeBlock(#"type User { name: String }

page Home(path: "/", title: "Home", description: "The home page.") {
    state user = User(name: "Sam")
    state items: [String] = ["a", "b", "c"]
    Heading(t("welcome", { name: user.name })).h1
    Text(t("items", { count: items.length }))
    Button(t("actions.save")).primary
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- `t(\"key\")` is the string for the current locale, reactive: every call re-evaluates when the locale changes.\n- `t(\"key\", \{ name: value \})` fills `\{name\}` placeholders from the map.\n- A `count` picks the plural form the locale's rules give it — CLDR's rules, which the browser's `Intl.PluralRules` follows too: Arabic has `zero`, `one`, `two`, `few`, `many` and `other`; English `one` and `other`; Japanese only `other`. Exactly none is `key.zero` wherever the file has one, in any language, so English can say \"No files\" rather than \"0 files\". Falls back to `key.other`, then `key`.\n- A key's forms all come from one language: the reader's, when its file has the key at all, otherwise the default locale's — never one form from each.\n- The static paint resolves `t` for the default locale at build time, by the same rules, so the pre-rendered HTML is already in the default language, its counts in that language's forms.")

        Heading("Switching the locale", id: "switching-the-locale").h2

        CodeBlock(#"app {
    state chosen = "en"
    Navbar {
        Navbar.Brand { Link("i18n", to: "/") }
        Navbar.Links {
            Link(t("nav.home"), to: "/")
            Link(t("nav.about"), to: "/about")
        }
        Navbar.Actions {
            Select(bind: chosen, label: t("nav.language")) {
                on change { setLocale(chosen) }
                Select.Option("English", value: "en")
                Select.Option("العربية", value: "ar")
                Select.Option("Español", value: "es")
            }
        }
    }
    Router
}

page Home(path: "/") {
    Text("Locale: {locale}, direction: {dir}")
    if locale == "ar" { Text("النسخة العربية") }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`setLocale(\"ar\")` switches; `locale` is the current code and `dir` is `\"ltr\"` or `\"rtl\"`. On a switch, `<html lang>` and `<html dir>` are set. A locale is right to left when its language is — Arabic, Hebrew, Persian, Urdu, Pashto, Sindhi, Central Kurdish, Uyghur, Yiddish, Dhivehi, Kashmiri, Syriac, Aramaic and N'Ko — so `ar-EG` is too. A site whose default locale is one of them is written `<html lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">` from the start, before any script runs. `meta.lang` names a region of the default locale's language (`en-GB` for `en`); otherwise the default locale is the page's language. The built-ins use logical CSS properties (`margin-inline-start`, not `margin-left`), so layouts mirror without work on your side; do the same in your own `style` blocks.\n\nAn `app` block may declare `state` like a page, as `chosen` above.")

        Heading("Which locale a page opens in", id: "which-locale-a-page-opens-in").h2

        Markdown("A page opens in `default_locale`, unless its address carries `?lang=<code>` for one of the configured locales — `/about?lang=ar` opens the Arabic page. That is the address the build gives each language in the `hreflang` alternates it writes for search engines, so a reader who arrives from a search result in Arabic reads Arabic.\n\nThe runtime does not guess from the browser's language, but it remembers a choice. `setLocale(code)` keeps the code in the reader's `localStorage`, under `wf:locale`, and every page loaded after that opens in it, on this visit and the next; choosing `default_locale` again forgets it. A `?lang=` address opens its page in that language and leaves the kept choice alone. So a static site, which loads each page afresh, keeps the reader's language from page to page, and a language switch is a button that calls `setLocale`:")

        CodeBlock(#"app {
    Navbar {
        Navbar.Brand { Link("i18n", to: "/") }
        Navbar.Actions {
            Button("English") { on click { setLocale("en") } }
            Button("العربية") { on click { setLocale("ar") } }
        }
    }
    Router
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Nothing is kept until the reader chooses, so a first visit, and a search engine's crawler, still get the `?lang=` locale or the default. To start a first visit from the browser's language, keep whether the reader has chosen in a store with `persist`, and until they have, apply `setLocale(navigator.language.slice(0, 2))` from an `effect` in the app.\n\nA static build pre-renders every page once, in the default locale; another locale is applied as the page loads. For search engines that is what the `?lang=` alternates are for.")

        Heading("Numbers, dates and the locale", id: "numbers-dates-and-the-locale").h2

        Markdown("`format` and `ago` follow the current locale ([chapter 14](/docs/guide/expressions#format-and-ago)): `format(1234.5, .currency, \"EUR\")` is `€1,234.50` in `en`, `1.234,50 €` in `de`; `ago(date)` says \"il y a 3 jours\" in `fr`. The static paint uses a table of the common locales' separators and currency symbols; the live page uses `Intl` and corrects any difference.")

        Heading("Translating a Markdown page or a .md page", id: "translating-a-markdown-page-or-a-md-page").h2

        Markdown("Text inside a `.md` page is not passed through `t`; keep one Markdown file per locale and route by a `:locale` parameter, or keep the long-form text in the translation file under a key and render `Markdown(t(\"about.body\"))`.")

        Heading("Checklist", id: "checklist").h2

        Markdown("- Every visible string goes through `t`, including `label:`, `placeholder:` and `aria-label:` values.\n- Never build a sentence from parts (`t(\"x\") + name`); use a placeholder so a translator can reorder it.\n- Give every count its plural forms, even in English, and a `zero` where none reads better as a sentence.\n- Test with an RTL locale once: alignment, icons that imply direction (arrows), any `left`/`right` in your own CSS, and a Latin code or figure inside Arabic text, which needs a left-to-right run of its own (`direction: ltr; unicode-bidi: isolate`) to keep its parts in order.")

        Heading("The translations as a table", id: "the-translations-as-a-table").h2

        Markdown("`wf translations` lists each locale with the plural forms its rules use and how many keys it lacks, a form it lacks, or a placeholder that differs from the default locale's; `--missing ar` lists what Arabic lacks and `--unused` the keys nothing names; `--json` is the whole table, each key with its message in every locale and every place a `t()` names it. `--plurals` prints CLDR's rules: every locale's forms. The library has it as `webfluent::i18n::table`, which also writes one message back into its file with every other byte as it was.")
    }
}
